223	\section{Numerical illustration}
224	
225	\Cref{ch:numerics} compares the theorem with an archived numerical snapshot of exact computations of $V_f(x,y)$ for $f(t)=t^2+1$, $t^3+2$ and $2t+1$, for $2.5\cdot10^5\le x\le 2.56\cdot10^8$ and $f(y)/x\in[0.005,1]$; the computations are repeated by the script \texttt{reproduce.py} accompanying this document. The constants are
226	\[
227	K_{t^2+1}=\KTwo,\qquad K_{t^3+2}=\KThree,\qquad K_{2t+1}=\KLin,
228	\]
229	computed from \eqref{eq:K-intro} with primes up to $\Pmax$. At the largest $x$ the data agree with the profile $x^2F_f(x/Y)$ to within a fraction of one per cent for $t^2+1$ and $2t+1$, and to within a few per cent for $t^3+2$, where $y$ is only a few hundred. The snapshot's empirical extrapolation to $r\to0$ is consistent with $K_f$ within its heuristic uncertainty envelope. \Cref{sec:extrapolation} splits its offset from $K_f$ into a part due to the fitting model itself and a part due to the difference between data and profile at finite $x$. The numbers are evidence, not proof; \cref{ch:numerics} explains the method and its limitations.
